{
  "sn42.7:0.1": "Linked Discourses 42.7 ",
  "sn42.7:0.2": "1. Chiefs ",
  "sn42.7:0.3": "The Simile of the Field ",
  "sn42.7:1.1": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Nāḷandā in Pāvārika’s Mango Grove. ",
  "sn42.7:1.2": "Then Asibandhaka’s son the chief went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: ",
  "sn42.7:1.3": "“Sir, doesn’t the Buddha live full of sympathy for all living beings?” ",
  "sn42.7:1.4": "“Yes, chief.” ",
  "sn42.7:1.5": "“So why then, sir, do you teach some people thoroughly and others less thoroughly?” ",
  "sn42.7:1.6": "“Well then, chief, I’ll ask you about this in return, and you can answer as you like. ",
  "sn42.7:1.7": "What do you think? Suppose a farmer has three fields: one is good, one is average, and one is poor—bad ground of sand and salt. ",
  "sn42.7:1.8": "What do you think? When that farmer wants to plant seeds, where would he plant them first: the good field, the average one, or the poor one?” ",
  "sn42.7:1.9": "“Sir, he’d plant them first in the good field, then the average, then he may or may not plant seed in the poor field. ",
  "sn42.7:1.10": "Why is that? ",
  "sn42.7:1.11": "Because at least it can be fodder for the cattle.” ",
  "sn42.7:2.1": "“To me, the monks and nuns are like the good field. ",
  "sn42.7:2.2": "I teach them the Dhamma that’s good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaningful and well-phrased. And I reveal a spiritual practice that’s entirely full